course: dictionary
lesson: 390
title: Apiece: each one
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Apiece means for each one. It tells you that the same amount goes to every single person or thing. In «The Badger gives the hedgehogs sixpence apiece and a pat on the head», every hedgehog gets sixpence, not one sixpence shared between them.
explain: How do you spot it? Ask: is the amount given to each one? Then try swapping in the word each. «Sixpence each» still makes sense, so apiece means each.
explain: Apiece often comes after the amount, as in «two apiece» or «a dollar apiece». Shops use it to show a price for each item: «The apples cost 30 cents apiece» means one apple costs 30 cents.
fact: Apiece means for each one, or each.
fact: Apiece is an adverb, and it usually comes after the amount: «two apiece», «a dollar apiece».
fact: «The apples cost 30 cents apiece» means each apple costs 30 cents.
fact: A good check is to swap in each and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Apiece is about sharing out the same amount for every one, so it does not mean the total.
exercise: «The Badger gives the hedgehogs sixpence apiece.» What does apiece mean? | Given in the morning | Very kind and generous | Only one in total | *For each one
exercise: The sign says «The apples cost 30 cents apiece.» What does this tell you? | The apples are free | All the apples together cost 30 cents | The apples are sour | *Each apple costs 30 cents
exercise: Which word can swap in for apiece in «two apiece»? | never | only | *each | together
test: The tickets are «five dollars apiece». How much does one ticket cost? | Ten dollars | Half a dollar | *Five dollars | Fifty dollars in total
test: «The Badger gives the hedgehogs sixpence apiece.» Do the hedgehogs share one sixpence? | Yes, the Badger keeps it all | *No, each hedgehog gets sixpence | Yes, they share one sixpence | No, nobody gets any money
exam: Mum bought two sandwiches apiece for the trip. What does this mean? | Everyone shared just two sandwiches | Two sandwiches were left over | *Each person got two sandwiches | Mum bought no sandwiches at all
